  "notes": "The causative form of {悩|なや}む (to worry, to be troubled). While grammatically a causative, {悩|なや}ませる has become a standard transitive verb in its own right and is commonly used with inanimate subjects (problems, pain, weather, etc.) that cause trouble.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- ～が（{人|ひと}）を{悩|なや}ませる — something troubles someone\n- ～に{悩|なや}ませられる — to be troubled by (passive of causative)\n- {長年|ながねん}{悩|なや}ませてきた — has troubled for many years\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {困|こま}らせる — to cause difficulty for someone (more practical/concrete problems)\n- {苦|くる}しめる — to cause suffering, to torment (stronger, more painful)\n- {悩|なや}む — to worry, to be troubled (intransitive base verb)",
